49.8 A sub-expression is a value
Writing a sentence as plain function calls makes one law easy to see. A piece of a sentence is an ordinary JavaScript value, so you can give it a name, use it again, and hand it to a function. It means the same thing in every sentence that holds it:


const piled = layer(point, bin, stack);
console.log(String(plot(data(gapminder_2007), piled, x(col.life))));
&lt;gog plot: point on data&gt;


Printing a sentence gives one line naming its mark and its table, not the picture. piled could go into a second sentence unchanged, and it would mean the same three words there. That law is Compositional Invariance, the sixth of the nine laws, and here the language provides it rather than the binding.

49.9 Reading from a database
Node.js, where JavaScript runs outside a browser, has no database standard, so query() accepts a connection by the methods it has rather than by its type. A connection that can prepare a statement and return all its rows works:
import { DatabaseSync } from "node:sqlite";
import { plot, data, query, bar, x, y, col, render_svg } from "grammar-of-graphics";

const con = new DatabaseSync("sales.db");
render_svg(plot(query(con, "SELECT status, revenue FROM orders"),
  bar, x(col.status), y(col.revenue)));
node:sqlite is built into Node.js. Version 22 needs a flag to turn it on, and from version 23.4 it is on already, so that example needs nothing installed. better-sqlite3 has the same shape and works the same way.

49.9.1 Asynchronous drivers are refused
Not every driver answers that way. render_svg() is synchronous: it returns the SVG rather than a Promise, which is what lets a plot be written inline in an expression. A driver whose query returns a Promise cannot be awaited inside a synchronous function.
So pg and mysql2 do not work with query(), and gog says so rather than letting a Promise reach the engine. The refusal gives the direction: fetch the rows yourself, then bind them as an ordinary table.
const { rows } = await con.query("SELECT status, revenue FROM orders");
render_svg(plot(data(rows), bar, x(col.status), y(col.revenue)));
That works with every driver, asynchronous ones included. data() takes the rows an asynchronous driver returns, and the sentence after it is unchanged. Everywhere else a language rule changes only how a sentence is spelled. This is the one place where it changes what a sentence can do, and it costs one await.

49.11 A plot for a web page: html_block()
The string render_svg() returns is the drawing and nothing else. For a web page, html_block() returns the same drawing with two things added: the line of controls under the picture, and, for some plots, a copy of the engine compiled for the browser. That copy redraws the plot as you turn or brush it. Four kinds of plot need it: a plot in the cube, a plot on the globe, a turned network, and a plot with a brush. Those are the plots of Space, Globe, Network and Selection.


const cube = plot(data(gapminder_2007), point,
                  x(col.gdp), y(col.life), z(col.population));
console.log(html_block(cube).split("\n")[0]);
&lt;div class="gog-plot" id="gog-0000ygbr50" style="text-align:center;"&gt;


The function returns HTML as a string. Put it in a page, a notebook cell, or a dashboard panel. The drawing comes first in that string, so a reader sees the plot before the copy of the engine has finished loading. That copy is compiled to WebAssembly, and a page whose security policy forbids WebAssembly still shows the plot. What stops working is the dragging: the cube and the globe no longer turn, and the brush no longer moves.
R, Python and Julia never call this function. A notebook in those languages asks the plot object how it would be displayed, and each binding answers: R with repr_html, Python with _repr_html_, Julia with a show method. JavaScript has no such request to answer, so here it is a function you call.
